# Pre-registration of the matrix, split, margin and verdict rules (experiment-repo)

Written by `experiment-repo` in answer to `experiment-repo`#2 (from `idea-repo` Brief rev. 3). The commit that adds this file
(see `git log prereg/PREREG.md`) dates it. **No predictive-error result of any method has been computed when this file is
committed.** What has been looked at: file counts, the number of models/tasks/cells that survive each rule, group sizes and
release-date coverage (structure only). The coverage thresholds R7 were chosen after seeing that count table
(grid of tt in {30..100} x tm in {15..40}); they are not tuned on any score model. Later changes are listed only under
"Deviations" at the end, each with date and reason.

## A. Data (pinned)
`embeddings-benchmark/results` at commit `6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383` (2026-03-31), CC0-1.0; 548 model dirs, of which
536 hold task files. Task metadata (type, declared eval splits, modality) from `mteb==2.27.24` via `mteb.get_task(name)` (metadata only).
Scores are `main_score` x 100 ("raw points", 0-100 scale; a few are negative).

## B. Matrix rules (code: `code/mteb_cells.py`, `code/03_build_matrix.py`)
- **R1 one revision per model.** Within a model directory keep the revision directory with the most distinct text-task files; ties -> latest
  `release_date` in its `model_meta.json`, then lexicographically smallest directory name. Revisions are never pooled.
- **R2 benchmark = task.** Only tasks whose mteb modality set is exactly {text}. A benchmark is one task file (task name). Tasks unknown to mteb
  metadata are dropped (12 names, none text).
- **R3 split.** Per (model, task) the first of `test, test.full, validation, validation.full, dev, devtest, default` that is declared in the task's
  `eval_splits` and present. `train` is never used.
- **R4 task version.** A cell is kept only if its `dataset_revision` equals the task's modal `dataset_revision` over models.
- **R5 subsets/languages.** The task's canonical subset set is its modal set of `hf_subset` names over models; a cell is kept iff the model reports a
  superset of it; its score is the mean of `main_score` over the canonical subsets (languages averaged). `mteb_version` of the producing run is
  NOT controlled (stated limitation; sensitivity is a secondary analysis).
- **R6 dedup.** Drop config variants (`_(` in the directory name, e.g. `_(output_dtype=int8)`); of model directories with the same lower-cased
  `model_meta` name keep the one with most cells.
- **R7 coverage filter (iterated to a fixed point).** tasks observed for >= 50 models, models with >= 30 tasks.
- **Group.** org = the part of the directory name before `__`, lower-cased, non-alphanumerics removed, spelling aliases
  `bytedanceseed->bytedance`, `sbert->sentencetransformers` (defined in `mteb_cells.py::ORG_ALIAS`); no other merging (fine-tunes of a third party's
  base model under a different org are NOT merged: limitation, leakage across groups cannot be excluded).
- **Realised shape** (`data/coverage_filter.csv`, `data/matrix_summary.json`): **238 models x 328 tasks, 45,775 cells, density 0.586, 83 org groups**;
  largest group 12 models; run-set size per model min 30, median 221.5; 166 models have >= 109 cells. Task types: Retrieval 117, Classification 73,
  Clustering 56, STS 28, PairClassification 17, Reranking 13, BitextMining 10, MultilabelClassification 8, InstructionReranking 3, Summarization 3.
  Gate G1 (>=100 x >=30, k>=8 possible) is met. 11 of the 238 models have no release date.

## C. Splits (all fitting - standardisation, factor model, ranks, selection, margins - on training models only)
- **G (primary): group hold-out.** The 83 org groups are assigned to 10 folds (seed 20261126, greedy by group size so folds have similar numbers of models);
  every model is held out exactly once, the number of held-out groups with each CI is 83 (>= 20, so CIs are not "indicative" by G4). Inner CV for
  hyperparameters: 5 group folds over training models.
- **T (second primary): temporal.** Train = dated models released on or before the 0.70-quantile release date of the dated models, test = later dated models;
  undated models are excluded from both. Org overlap between train and test is allowed and reported (number of test models whose org is in training).
- **S (extrapolation to the strongest): strength.** Strength = model offset a_m of the additive fit y_mj = mu_j + a_m (alternating LS on all observed cells; used only
  to assign the split). Train = lowest 75% by a_m, test = top 25%.
- **LOMO** (leave one model out) only as sensitivity, reported with that label.
- Standardisation per task from training cells (mean, sd); raw-point errors are reported after back-transform.

## D. Revealed set, k grid, targets
- For held-out model m with run set R_m (benchmarks it has), a selection rule picks S subset of R_m, |S|=k; targets T_m(k) = R_m \ S (its remaining observed cells); error on T_m(k).
- **k grid** `K = {1,2,3,5,8,12,20,32,50,80,109}`, 109 = floor(N/3) with N=328; k/N shown. Models with |R_m| <= k are excluded at that k; the count excluded per k is reported.
  Headline curve also on fixed subset A (models with >= 20 cells, k<=20: all 238) and fixed subset B (models with >= 109+1 cells, whole grid).
  k > N/3 only as a labelled supplement.
- **Primary contrast** S6+P5 vs S0+P5 (random-k, same inference), paired over held-out models, at k in {1,2,3,5,8,12,20} (+ the longer grid as secondary), both on split G (and T as the second primary, reported separately).
  S0 = mean over 50 random draws per (model, k) (seeded); the draw-to-draw sd of the mean-over-models error is reported.
- Everything else (S1-S5, S7, P0-P4, P6, split S, conditions) is secondary; multiplicity: no p-values are used for secondary items; CIs are descriptive.

## E. Metrics
Per held-out model: RMSE over targets in raw points ("model RMSE"); standardised RMSE; headline numbers are the **mean over held-out models of model RMSE** with 90% group-bootstrap CI (resample org groups with replacement, 5000 reps).
Also MAE (raw), per-model Spearman across its targets, rank agreement among strong models (top-quartile by a_m), per-task error, error vs median adjacent-model gap of the task.

## F. Margins (computed per outer fold from training models only; the numbers are committed in `prereg/delta_by_fold.csv` before any contrast is run)
- gap_j = median over adjacent pairs of training models sorted by their score on task j (models with the cell).
- **all-others floor** floor_j = RMSE (raw points) of leave-one-benchmark-out prediction for training models' cells of task j, from all of that model's other cells, by P5 with
  parameters fit on the other training groups (5 group folds) and rank chosen by that CV.
- **delta_j = min(0.5 gap_j, 0.25 floor_j)** (raw points). Aggregate margin used for the contrast and for epsilon:
  **delta = mean of delta_j over the evaluated target cells** (pooled across folds). Delta (meaningful gain) = delta.
- **Verdict per k** on the paired difference d = mean_m(RMSE_S0 - RMSE_S6) (positive = design better), 90% group-bootstrap CI [lo, hi]:
  *equivalent* if -delta < lo and hi < +delta; *design helps* if lo > +delta; *random better* if hi < -delta; otherwise *undecided* (never reported as a tie).
- **k\*(epsilon).** Because delta <= 0.25 floor, an absolute error <= delta is unreachable; the criterion is therefore on the excess over the all-others floor of the same model and targets:
  excess(k) = mean_m [RMSE_m(k) - floor_m(k)], with floor_m(k) the RMSE on T_m(k) of leave-one-benchmark-out prediction from all other cells of m. k\*(epsilon) = smallest k in the grid with excess(k) <= epsilon,
  epsilon in {delta, 2 delta}; empirical k\* with group-bootstrap CI (smallest k whose bootstrap excess <= epsilon, per replicate); predicted k\*(epsilon) from T1 using the training-fit
  loadings/noise (predicted excess = sqrt(floor^2 + design(k)) - floor with floor^2 the model's expected noise variance). "Rule useful" only if the empirical k\* lies inside or within one grid step of the predicted band.
  (This redefinition of "error <= epsilon" is a decision of experiment-repo, flagged to idea-repo in #2.)

## G. Model, rank, selection (fixed before results)
- P5 = factor analysis y_mj = mu_j + v_j' z_m + e_mj, e ~ N(0, psi_j), z ~ N(0, I), EM on observed cells of the standardised training matrix (psi_j floor 1e-2, 300 iterations or relative LL change < 1e-6);
  new-model inference = posterior mean of z given the revealed cells. Rank r in {1,2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24} chosen by inner group CV, at the reveal size k of the evaluation (inner validation model reveals k random cells, predicts the rest of its cells).
- Effective rank (H1), defined before results: (i) parallel analysis (Horn; per-column permutation of observed entries, pairwise-complete correlation, 95th percentile of 200 permutations), (ii) number of eigenvalues above the
  Marchenko-Pastur noise edge with sigma^2 estimated from the lower half of the spectrum, (iii) CV-selected rank above; sensitivity to the imputation of missing cells (EM-FA imputation vs column-mean) reported.
- S6 = greedy maximiser of F(S) = tr(V Sigma_post(S) V') over all N benchmarks, candidates restricted to R_m (Sherman-Morrison gains); variants (unrun-only target; weighted) secondary. Greedy checked vs exhaustive/swap search in small cases.

## H. Baselines: status as to "run as published" (also sent to `idea-repo`)
Run as published: S0, S1, S2, S5 (k-medoids on 1-|corr| with pairwise-complete correlation), P0, P1, P3 (kNN), P4 (Soft-Impute).
Adaptations (each documented in the paper's appendix): **S3** greedy CV wrapper - cost forces a closed-form P5 evaluation on training rows, k<=32 only;
**S4** pivoted-QR/leverage needs a complete matrix - applied to the EM-FA completed matrix; **S7** Golbandi decision-tree interview is defined for discrete ratings - run as a continuous-score kNN/tree interview;
**P2** ridge with missing training cells - EM-completed training matrix; **P6** Ruan et al. observational scaling needs complete base-benchmark rows and (Sloth) compute/family covariates, which are not available for a new model with k scores - run as PC regression on the P5 latent scores with a sigmoid link
for bounded targets, i.e. NOT as published. **S8** exhaustive oracle needs N<=10 matrices (Open LLM LB N=6, HELM Lite N=10) - run only in the transfer check; on MTEB the greedy-vs-exhaustive check is done on random size-12/16 task subsets.
Cost-aware S6: MTEB cells carry `evaluation_time` (kept in `cells`); per-task cost = median over models; used only if coverage of `evaluation_time` is adequate, else dropped (will be stated).

## I. Reproducibility
Seeds: outer folds 20261126; random-k draws seed = hash(model, k, 20261126); EM init seed 0. Software versions in `requirements.lock` (to be added). Command order in `README.md`.

## Addendum 1 (2026-11-26, written after stage A margins, before any contrast or error curve is computed)
Stage A (code/04_states.sh, state.py: inner-CV ranks, floors, margins) was run on all folds; `prereg/delta_by_fold.csv` holds the per-fold, per-task numbers. Pooled over folds the
margin is tiny relative to the floor: mean gap_j ~ 0.21 raw points (238 models on a 0-100 scale), mean all-others floor ~ 4.6 points, so delta = mean min(0.5 gap, 0.25 floor) ~ 0.10 points
(the gap term always binds). The rules of section F are NOT changed and remain the pre-set primary margins. Because a +/-0.1-point equivalence band may be narrower than any attainable CI, two
secondary margins are declared now so that the verdict does not depend on a post-hoc choice: **delta_floor = 0.25 x floor_j** (aggregated like delta; about 1.1 points) and **delta_floor/2**. Verdicts and
k*(epsilon) are reported for the primary delta first and for delta_floor next to it; epsilon in {delta, 2 delta, delta_floor, 2 delta_floor}. Nothing else is added.

